## #618 first divergence ROOT-CAUSED and FIXED — `Color.parseColor` #AARRGGBB signed-overflow

This resolves divergence #1 from the F-DROID-APK batch receipt above (the `IllegalArgumentException (Unknown color: #DE000000)`). It was **not** a secondary formatting wrapper — it is a genuine engine bug in `parseColor` itself.

### What was broken
`android.graphics.Color.parseColor` accumulated the hex body into a **signed `int32_t`**:

```cpp
auto hexv = [](const std::string& h) -> int32_t { int32_t v = 0; ... v = (v<<4)|d; return v; };
...
} else if (s.size() == 9 && s[0] == '#') {
    int32_t argb = hexv(s.substr(1));
    if (argb >= 0) { c = argb; ok = true; }   // ← rejects every alpha >= 0x80
}
```

For any `#AARRGGBB` whose alpha nibble is `>= 0x8`, the 32-bit value overflows negative, so `argb >= 0` is false and the value is rejected as `Unknown color`. `#DE000000` (`0xDE000000` = `-570425344`), `#FF000000` (opaque black), and `#80000000` all fail.

### Root cause (authoritative)
Decompiled `Color.parseColor` from the repo's own `tools/android-34.jar`:

```
